00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.800
So one of things that I
think we should highlight

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.830
is the Google Cloud TPUs.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:35.100
So a TPU is like a CPU or a
GPU But CPU uses for computing

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.310
and G is for graphics,
T is for Tensor Flow.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.600
And they were designed a
long time ago actually.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.120
But we finally have them
available on Google Cloud.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.870
And they're pretty
amazing because they're

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:54.429
able to provide 180 terabytes--
no, not terabytes, teraflops.

00:01:54.429 --> 00:01:55.470
MARK MANDEL: That's more.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:56.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That is a lot.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:57.407
MARK MANDEL: And those
are different numbers.

00:01:57.407 --> 00:01:59.615
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's a
completely different thing.

00:01:59.615 --> 00:02:03.300
And a FLOP, it is not
something that falls.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.630
It is floating
operation, so FLOPS

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.110
is floating
operations per second,

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:12.780
and 180 terabytes
is a lot of them.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.790
And on top of that,
they're connected

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:18.540
to memory that is accessible
through a very, very

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.070
large bandwidth.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.350
You can communicate
with the memory

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:26.220
at 12.5 gigabytes per
second, which is crazy.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:26.970
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.553
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
yeah, you're going

00:02:28.553 --> 00:02:31.620
to be able to do some
really cool processing.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.680
I think that for
machine learning,

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.190
this is going to be huge.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.130
If you're running any
TensorFlow or probably

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:42.120
any other processing that makes
sense to run as TensorFlow,

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.300
machine learning, image
processing probably also helps.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:45.870
I don't know.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:46.900
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it's pretty--

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.691
