00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:31.800
let them log in with Google,
Twitter, or GitHub, et cetera.

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.720
Username and password
or even connect

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:35.980
with your own existing
user account system.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:38.396
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, that
is one of my favorite features

00:09:38.396 --> 00:09:38.969
by the way.

00:09:38.969 --> 00:09:41.260
JAMES TAMPLIN: Yeah, no
messing with [INAUDIBLE],, it's

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:42.270
fantastic.

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:43.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:44.961
JAMES TAMPLIN: So we
had a lot of requests.

00:09:44.961 --> 00:09:47.840
Especially from our users
in emerging markets,

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:49.590
where potentially they
don't have an email

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:51.270
address, or even in
developed markets

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:54.240
where there's just far
less friction with logging

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:55.870
in with the phone number.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:10:00.090
And so last week we announced
that this is now possible.

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:03.360
So you can just prompt
a user to log in,

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:06.400
they enter their phone number,
we send them a text message.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.860
And if you are on
Android, we even

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:10.630
do some pretty cool
special source,

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:14.020
where we'll automatically
read the text message

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:16.090
and log you in for a very,
very frictionless sign

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:17.260
up experience.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:19.931
So yeah, it's, again,
you want your users

00:10:19.931 --> 00:10:21.430
to be able to get
into applications,

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:24.006
start using the functionality
as quickly as possible.

00:10:24.006 --> 00:10:25.630
And this is one of
the ways where we're

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:28.770
making that happen for you

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:31.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So, OK, so
let's pick the second favorite.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:33.109
Why don't you go now, Andrew.

00:10:33.109 --> 00:10:35.649
ANDREW LEE: You know, I have to
go with the Hosting and Cloud

00:10:35.649 --> 00:10:36.604
Functions integration.

00:10:36.604 --> 00:10:38.020
This is something
I actually spoke

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:39.550
about, if you go
look on YouTube,

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:41.980
I gave a talk last Wednesday
talking about Firebase

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:45.520
and Google Cloud and our
Firebase Hosting plus Cloud

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.400
Functions integration
really opens up

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.180
an entirely new set
of possibilities

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:52.480
for using Firebase Hosting
and now for dynamic content.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.760
For years we've done a great
job serving static assets

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:57.220
but there's a bunch
of limitations

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:59.050
around have static assets only.

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:02.650
And you can now build
really any sort web app

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:04.480
very easily without
having to worry

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.990
about the servers or scaling
using our Cloud Functions

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:08.891
integration.

00:11:08.891 --> 00:11:10.390
MARK MANDEL: So now
I'm curious too,

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:13.270
because obviously you've had
integration with the Firebase

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:15.010
Realtime Database.

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:17.650
What functionality does that
give you above and beyond

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:20.560
like building a single page web
app using the Firebase Realtime

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:23.484
Database using Cloud Functions
on the web with the hosting?

00:11:23.484 --> 00:11:24.650
What's the difference there?

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:26.380
ANDREW LEE: So it
turns out that there's

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:27.796
a number of very
common things you

00:11:27.796 --> 00:11:31.180
might want to do that aren't
actually very possible using

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:31.930
a single page app.

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:34.450
So a good example of this,
which I demo-ed last Wednesday,

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:35.380
is social sharing.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:39.439
When you say, tweet
out a link to your app,

00:11:39.439 --> 00:11:41.230
Twitter is actually
going to go and they're

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:42.280
going to scrape that
page, and they're

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:43.988
going to pull off the
images so that they

00:11:43.988 --> 00:11:46.354
can show a nice picture
for the user in that tweet.

00:11:46.354 --> 00:11:47.770
And if you have a
single page app,

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:49.330
where the JavaScript
is doing the rendering,

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:50.720
Twitter might not be
able to pick that up.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:52.390
And this is actually
a very common pattern

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:54.723
around the internet where if
you want someone else to be

00:11:54.723 --> 00:11:56.530
looking at your page
and parsing the HTML,

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:58.090
it's much harder
if it's JavaScript.

00:11:58.090 --> 00:11:59.080
And so you could actually
do that rendering

00:11:59.080 --> 00:11:59.940
on our server side.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:02.440
Do a pre-render and show that.

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:04.510
Another reason is
actually for performance.

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:07.680
For Firebase Hosting,
we provide a CDN

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:09.910
that caches your results
all over the world

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:12.742
and if you have a page
that's maybe something

00:12:12.742 --> 00:12:14.950
that you want to render very
quickly on mobile phone,

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:17.990
or something that you want to
be like very high performance,

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:20.380
high scale, but for which
you want dynamic content,

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:23.560
you can use the Cloud
Functions to do your rendering.

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:26.290
And then you can use Firebase
Hosting to cache those results

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:27.350
at the edge.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:31.330
And provide very quick response
without needing the end user's

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:33.657
device to download the page,
connect to the database,

00:12:33.657 --> 00:12:35.740
download the data, and
then render things locally.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:37.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so
what does that look like

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:39.372
from the developer experience?

00:12:39.372 --> 00:12:41.080
You have, the static
hosting, so you just

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:43.180
have like HTML and some
JavaScript in there,

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:45.849
and then you deploy
those functions,

00:12:45.849 --> 00:12:47.890
and then you call them,
what does this look like?

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:49.973
ANDREW LEE: Yeah, there's
really two halves to it.

00:12:49.973 --> 00:12:52.660
So part of it is setting up
the Cloud Function itself,

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:53.630
which is pretty simple.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:55.750
You can use the Firebase
SDK for Cloud Functions

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:58.870
and the Firebase Command
Line Tool to write your code.

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:01.152
It's just node, so you can
use the whole node package

00:13:01.152 --> 00:13:02.860
ecosystem, including
things like Express.

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:05.890
And we've actually built the
SDK to plug nicely into Express,

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:07.960
use the same types of
objects and things,

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:09.620
so you can get
going very quickly.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:11.620
So in the case of hosting
[INAUDIBLE] functions,

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:14.944
you'd probably write some code
to render a page with Express

00:13:14.944 --> 00:13:17.110
and then you'd go over to
your hosting configuration

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:21.460
and you'd connect certain paths,
certain urls over to functions.

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:23.710
And you'd say, with the
rewrite rule, you'd say,

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:26.350
this path should actually
be served by this function.

00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:27.960
And then you say
Firebase Deploy,

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:29.560
we'll put the
functions up there,

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:31.810
we'll send that
configuration to hosting,

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.920
and we'll tie them together
for you behind the scenes.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:35.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.770
I actually saw your
talk at Google I/O.

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:38.770
We'll have a link to it.

00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:39.519
It is really good.

00:13:39.519 --> 00:13:42.310
I really love the fact that--

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:44.710
normally, when you see
people like co-founders,

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:46.515
they don't tend to
write much code.

00:13:46.515 --> 00:13:47.890
So getting on
stage at Google I/O

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:50.290
and writing so much code,
like congratulations,

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:51.280
that was really good.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:53.779
ANDREW LEE: You know, I have
to admit, I had to kind of dust

00:13:53.779 --> 00:13:56.689
off my programming skills the
weekend before, including--

00:13:56.689 --> 00:13:58.480
this was the first kind
of meaningful thing

00:13:58.480 --> 00:13:59.710
I've done with TypeScript.

00:13:59.710 --> 00:14:00.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh cool.

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:02.740
ANDREW LEE: And so that's
kind of an exciting, fun thing

00:14:02.740 --> 00:14:04.820
for me to learn a new language.

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:05.800
So I really enjoyed it.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:06.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That was very good.

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:09.055
We'll have a link to that
video from the show notes.

00:14:09.055 --> 00:14:10.520
So definitely check it out.

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:12.340
Let's go for one more lunch.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:14.134
What is your other favorite?

00:14:14.134 --> 00:14:16.300
JAMES TAMPLIN: Sure so I
think my other favorite was

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:20.200
we started to open source our
SDKs, which developers have

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:21.744
been asking us to do for years.

00:14:21.744 --> 00:14:23.410
And it was a very
exciting moment for me

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.054
and I think most of the team.

00:14:25.054 --> 00:14:26.470
This is really
important because I

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:29.290
think it's a key part of the
Google Cloud Platform Strategy,

00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:30.850
is openness.

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:34.780
Leading the way with things
like Kubernetes and TensorFlow

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:38.060
and projects throughout
the rest of Google.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:40.270
So we were excited
to join in the fun.

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:43.329
So the big news here is
our iOS, our JavaScript,

00:14:43.329 --> 00:14:45.370
and our admin, which are
kind of our service side

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:47.920
SDKs are now open source.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:50.170
Specifically, the products
within them, those back end

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:54.340
service products that we were
talking about earlier are open.

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:56.110
And it's all on GitHub.

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:58.480
So our primary tool
chain is now GitHub.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:02.110
So you can go and go
to GitHub.com/Firebase,

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:05.150
check out those SDKs, pull
requests against them,

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:08.950
and generally look at kind of
what we're doing and contribute

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:09.450
to it.

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:12.030
And we'd love for you
to go and do that.

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:15.270
I think in the past couple
