00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:47.461
MARK MANDEL: COBOL.

00:16:47.461 --> 00:16:48.670
COBOL SDK.

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.540
JAMES TAMPLIN: Excellent
all right We'll

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:52.204
take that to the team.

00:16:52.204 --> 00:16:53.870
MARK MANDEL: We'll
go we'll go one more.

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:55.245
What's your final
favorite there?

00:16:55.245 --> 00:16:57.578
ANDREW LEE: I feel like we
didn't talk about Performance

00:16:57.578 --> 00:16:58.769
Monitoring yet, did we?

00:16:58.769 --> 00:16:59.810
I've got to mention that.

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:01.995
So this is the one new product.

00:17:01.995 --> 00:17:03.620
Everything else we've
talked about here

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:05.884
is features on existing
products or new integrations,

00:17:05.884 --> 00:17:07.550
but this is actually
a brand new product

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:11.480
from Firebase that lets
you, from your SDK,

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:14.619
do timings of network
requests and on anything else,

00:17:14.619 --> 00:17:16.369
really, that you want
in your application,

00:17:16.369 --> 00:17:18.160
to get a sense for how
your app is actually

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:19.190
performing in the wild.

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:22.490
And this is really important
because you, as a developer,

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:25.670
on the latest and greatest
phone on your Wi-Fi network

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:29.090
in your office, may not get a
good sense for what it actually

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:30.830
is like to be a
consumer on your app.

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:34.040
And this is the tool you really
need to be able to do that.

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:36.736
So this just launched
last week, it's brand new.

00:17:36.736 --> 00:17:38.360
We've gotten some
really great reviews.

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:39.910
There's already been a
number of large customers

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:41.284
that launched it
since the event.

00:17:41.284 --> 00:17:43.950
They heard about it and they put
it right in there right away.

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:46.850
And so I'm really excited to
see how this grows over time.

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:50.470
JAMES TAMPLIN: Yeah, and it
will give you stock traces,

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:56.440
it will give you percentiles
on your latency and network

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:56.940
requests.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:58.670
Yeah, it's a really cool tool.

00:17:58.670 --> 00:18:00.730
I think a team has
done a lot of iteration

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:02.480
over the past couple
of months and I think

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:04.200
the UI is really intuitive.

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:08.470
So it doesn't cost
anything; completely free.

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:12.090
Comes turned on when you
use the Firebase SDK.

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:15.650
So if you haven't yet,
throw that into your app

00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:17.030
and start going.

00:18:17.030 --> 00:18:18.130
It's pretty cool.

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:19.880
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
that sounds super cool.

