00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:04.910
And then, similarly, we've
done a bunch of work on Unity

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:05.467
and c++.

00:19:05.467 --> 00:19:07.050
And this actually
didn't launch at I/O

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:08.841
but it launched relatively
recently before,

00:19:08.841 --> 00:19:10.700
so I think it's
worth mentioning.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:13.310
But we're making a
big push into games

00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:15.210
and trying to address
those markets too.

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:16.400
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
it looks like you--

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:18.567
I mean, you expanded more
at I/O into games as well.

00:19:18.567 --> 00:19:21.066
You opened up Game Support and
Test Lab as well, didn't you?

00:19:21.066 --> 00:19:21.750
ANDREW LEE: Yep.

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:23.510
MARK MANDEL: And that's
super, super cool.

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:25.801
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So something
that is also pretty cool,

00:19:25.801 --> 00:19:28.730
we use it all the time for all
of our projects, specifically

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:31.880
for Google Cloud Platform
Podcasts, is Google Analytics.

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:34.520
And it helps
understand what people

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:37.910
are doing with your web apps.

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:40.730
And it seems that there
is a new thing, which

00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:43.710
is Google Analytics for
Firebase, which is specifically

00:19:43.710 --> 00:19:46.340
for the applications that
you can build with Firebase.

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:48.290
Could you tell a little
bit more about that?

00:19:48.290 --> 00:19:49.331
JAMES TAMPLIN: Certainly.

00:19:49.331 --> 00:19:51.230
So a year ago at I/O
2016, we announced

00:19:51.230 --> 00:19:54.200
a brand new analytics
product from Google

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:56.030
called Firebase Analytics.

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:59.990
In reality, the analytics
team helped us with this.

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:02.300
They did, you know,
98% of the work.

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:05.750
And and it's not actually a
brand new analytics product,

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:09.830
it's a native mobile
focused analytics product

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:11.560
from the Google Analytics team.

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:14.230
And it appears if you go
to analytics.google.com

00:20:14.230 --> 00:20:17.256
and, you know, it also has a
view in the Firebase console.

00:20:17.256 --> 00:20:19.130
And so we had a lot of
people over the course

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:21.440
of the last year being
like, which one do I use?

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:23.110
Google Analytics or
Firebase Analytics?

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:24.590
And we're like no, no, no.

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:25.790
It's all the same.

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:28.100
And so we're reflecting
that now by rebranding it

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:30.336
as Google Analytics
for Firebase.

00:20:30.336 --> 00:20:31.252
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:31.252 --> 00:20:33.830
So what is the difference?

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:35.950
You said that it's
specifically centered

00:20:35.950 --> 00:20:37.162
into mobile applications?

00:20:37.162 --> 00:20:38.120
JAMES TAMPLIN: Exactly.

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:41.560
So Google Analytics,
the original version

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:43.670
was built in a web
focused world where

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:45.620
you had these kind of
sessions and page views.

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:46.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:47.633
JAMES TAMPLIN: And
so Google Analytics

00:20:47.633 --> 00:20:49.610
for Firebase is,
since Firebase is

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:53.810
Google's mobile platform, has
iOS and Android SDKs number

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:54.730
one.

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:58.200
Number two is we've moved out
of that web page focused world

00:20:58.200 --> 00:20:59.840
into a mobile focused world.

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:03.480
A lot more event data
is being generated.

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:05.661
So what we do is we
provide free unlimited,

00:21:05.661 --> 00:21:07.160
unlike Google
Analytics where if you

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:08.120
hit a certain
number of events you

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:09.440
have to have to start paying.

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:12.830
And we do that by creating
what's called a rolling 30 day

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:13.640
base view.

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:17.570
So we basically keep every
single event for 30 days

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:20.450
and then we start aggregating.

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:23.180
And so that way where we're
able to provide this product

00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:25.460
for free to whatever scale.

00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:28.440
We have some of the biggest apps
in the world using the product.

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:30.140
And I think one of
the exciting things

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:33.170
that we also-- you know,
besides just the re-brand,

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:35.160
we announced a couple
of really cool things.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.910
One is support for
Android Instant Apps which

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:40.130
is the ability to use an
application without actually

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:42.582
having to install it
from the Play Store.

00:21:42.582 --> 00:21:44.540
So that's really cool if
you want introspection

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:45.320
to those apps.

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:48.530
And the other thing that I think
Google Cloud Platform customers

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:51.920
and developers are really
going to care about is we

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:55.490
partnered with the BigQuery
team to announce a 10 gigabyte

00:21:55.490 --> 00:21:57.540
free storage TF BigQuery.

00:21:57.540 --> 00:22:00.254
So now, you have to
enter a credit card,

00:22:00.254 --> 00:22:01.670
but once you have
that credit card

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:04.520
and you can export from
Google Analytics Firebase

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:07.730
directly into BigQuery to
do much richer querying.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:11.840
And as I said, after 30 days
we start doing some sampling

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:12.880
on that data.

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:15.290
And if you want to keep
it in its full fidelity

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:16.730
you can export it
out to BigQuery

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:19.594
and do all the querying
that your heart desires.

00:22:19.594 --> 00:22:22.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so that
means that actually now you

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:24.450
get the 10 gigabytes
of storage for free

00:22:24.450 --> 00:22:27.230
plus one terabyte
per month of queries.

00:22:27.230 --> 00:22:29.670
So that's a lot of processing.

00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:30.620
That's very cool.

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:32.000
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, that
gives people really

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:34.280
like a great opportunity
to play with the analytics

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:36.704
and see if they need
to use BigQuery.

00:22:36.704 --> 00:22:38.120
Just try it out
to see if it works

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:40.453
or not without having to take
some charge upfront, which

00:22:40.453 --> 00:22:41.460
is super nice.

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:43.960
ANDREW LEE: I'm excited about
it because the query is really

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:45.950
one of the hidden gems of GCP.

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:48.620
I don't know if
everyone understands

00:22:48.620 --> 00:22:50.060
how powerful tool
this really is,

00:22:50.060 --> 00:22:51.655
but it's really
really pretty amazing.

00:22:51.655 --> 00:22:53.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
It is for me, it

00:22:53.030 --> 00:22:55.760
is the best to
Google Cloud Platform

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:57.400
if you want to wow someone.

00:22:57.400 --> 00:23:00.260
Like you just demo
little, little things.

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:02.630
Like just processing
a couple of terabytes

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:05.580
of data in a second
and their like, what?

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:06.414
So that's very easy.

00:23:06.414 --> 00:23:08.288
JAMES TAMPLIN: So I have
actually an anecdote

00:23:08.288 --> 00:23:08.900
for everyone.

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:12.890
So we, prior to joining Google,
used another data warehouse

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:16.130
solution to store and process
all of our stats pipelines

00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:17.300
and analytics.

00:23:17.300 --> 00:23:18.770
And when we joined
Google we were

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:21.290
required to move over to
GCP infrastructure, which

00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:22.340
we did gladly.

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:25.370
And we moved from this
solution over to BigQuery.

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:30.222
And it decreased our query times
from about 60 seconds to 5.

00:23:30.222 --> 00:23:32.680
So you know, I'd be sitting
there, you know click a button,

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:34.430
wait 60 seconds, see
the graph come up,

00:23:34.430 --> 00:23:39.350
and it made me a lot
personally happier

00:23:39.350 --> 00:23:42.147
and it was fantastic for the
rest of the team as well.

00:23:42.147 --> 00:23:44.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I guess
especially if the queries

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:46.563
that you're running, you're
actually debugging it.

00:23:46.563 --> 00:23:49.220
And you're not sure if they're
going to give the good results.

00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:52.580
Having to wait for one minute
to make sure that this works

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:54.330
or not, that sounds like a lot.

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:56.000
Yeah.

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:57.367
Cool.

00:23:57.367 --> 00:23:58.950
MARK MANDEL: So
speaking of analytics,

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:00.140
there are a couple of
other things in here

00:24:00.140 --> 00:24:02.181
I thought looked really
interesting, particularly

00:24:02.181 --> 00:24:04.070
the Stream View
and the De-Bug View

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:06.320
to help people be able to
kind of see what's going on

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:07.278
inside their analytics.

00:24:07.278 --> 00:24:08.330
Can you talk about those?

00:24:08.330 --> 00:24:09.038
ANDREW LEE: Sure.

00:24:09.038 --> 00:24:11.025
So those actually
launched a little bit ago.

00:24:11.025 --> 00:24:13.400
They didn't come out at I/O
but I think they're exciting,

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:14.580
we're talking about--

00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:16.580
so these are a couple
of additional tabs

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:18.980
under the Google Analytics
for Firebase section

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:21.890
of the console,
that one, gives you

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:24.690
a better idea of how
people around the world

00:24:24.690 --> 00:24:25.940
are interacting with your app.

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:29.240
So it is kind of cool live map
showing what people are doing.

00:24:29.240 --> 00:24:31.580
And then the De-Bug
View is really

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:35.150
intended to help you during
the installation phase

00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:38.270
to get a sense for if your
app if your app is actually

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:39.650
correctly hooked
up to Analytics.

00:24:39.650 --> 00:24:41.420
So generally with
Analytics, there's

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:43.320
a bit of a delay between
when the analytics are logged

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:44.430
and when they're reported.

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:46.150
And this is for battery
efficiency reasons.

00:24:46.150 --> 00:24:47.790
We don't actually want to stream
everything down right away,

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:49.787
we want a kind of batch process.

00:24:49.787 --> 00:24:52.370
But you don't actually want to
do that while you're setting up

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:52.760
the app.

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:55.260
You want to be able to have a
real fast de-bug cycle, so you

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:58.000
use De-Bug View to
get a sense for,

00:24:58.000 --> 00:24:59.330
is my app configured correctly?

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:02.450
Am I logging the right
events, and then when you go

00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:04.414
live you're much more
battery efficient.

00:25:04.414 --> 00:25:05.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:06.470
We're kind of
running out of time

00:25:06.470 --> 00:25:08.300
but there's a question
that I want to ask,

00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:10.633
which is, OK so there was a
lot of cool things announced

00:25:10.633 --> 00:25:12.739
at Google I/O. What's next?

00:25:12.739 --> 00:25:14.780
JAMES TAMPLIN: I think
there's a couple of themes

00:25:14.780 --> 00:25:15.930
at a high level.

00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:18.290
The first theme is--

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:21.380
I think this is no secret
and Google is working

00:25:21.380 --> 00:25:22.780
on this across the board--

00:25:22.780 --> 00:25:25.380
is weaving machine
learning into our products.

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:27.470
So especially on the
growth side of Firebase,

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:30.830
being able to intelligently
recommend to developers actions

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:33.800
they can take to increase
their user base in the most

00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:36.300
intelligent number of steps.

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:37.550
So I think that's one big one.

00:25:37.550 --> 00:25:39.710
I think the other
big one to note,

00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:41.660
that your listeners
will be interested in,

00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:42.770
is deeper integration.

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:44.990
Continued integration with
Google Cloud Platform.

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:46.040
As Andrew mentioned
earlier, they

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:46.880
are the same under the hood.

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:49.070
You know, we share billing
accounts and projects

00:25:49.070 --> 00:25:51.290
and all of those pieces.

00:25:51.290 --> 00:25:54.170
And right now we have Cloud
Functions and Cloud Storage,

00:25:54.170 --> 00:25:56.750
which are shared between
the two platforms

00:25:56.750 --> 00:26:00.410
and you should expect more
exciting feature sharing

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:02.120
coming in the future.

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:05.602
And we've got a couple of things
in the pipeline that I cannot

00:26:05.602 --> 00:26:06.560
wait to show the world.

00:26:06.560 --> 00:26:08.090
And it's going to be
really, really cool.

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:08.965
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:26:08.965 --> 00:26:10.820
Well before we wrap
up, is there anything

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:13.370
we missed or something you
want to plug or mention?

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:14.660
Or anything really
going on that you think

00:26:14.660 --> 00:26:16.076
is particularly
exciting, you make

00:26:16.076 --> 00:26:17.327
sure the listeners hear about?

00:26:17.327 --> 00:26:19.784
ANDREW LEE: I want to remind
everyone about Cloud Functions

00:26:19.784 --> 00:26:20.830
because it's super cool.

00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:23.288
I know we kind of touched on
it with Hosting and Functions.

00:26:23.288 --> 00:26:25.530
This is actually a
new thing for us.

00:26:25.530 --> 00:26:28.220
We launched the Cloud
Functions for Firebase in March

00:26:28.220 --> 00:26:32.690
along with the public launch
of Cloud Functions in general,

00:26:32.690 --> 00:26:35.330
and it is really slick.

00:26:35.330 --> 00:26:36.770
So if you haven't
given the shot,

00:26:36.770 --> 00:26:39.680
it lets you hook up
little snippets of code

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:42.390
to events throughout
the Google ecosystem.

00:26:42.390 --> 00:26:44.930
So if you have a right
to your database,

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:47.270
if you have a
Pub/Sub event, if you

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:49.070
have a conversion
event and analytics,

00:26:49.070 --> 00:26:51.080
all these can trigger code.

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:53.790
And it's run in a
managed way by Google,

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:55.880
it's automatically
scaled by Google,

00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:57.860
and it makes it really
easy for you to get up

00:26:57.860 --> 00:26:59.540
and running without
having to worry

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:02.820
about operations and scaling
and all of those things.

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:05.390
So this actually ties
nicely into what's next,

00:27:05.390 --> 00:27:07.460
and I spoke about
this last Thursday

00:27:07.460 --> 00:27:09.470
actually in one of
the fireside chats.

00:27:09.470 --> 00:27:11.990
But I think you're
going to see a lot more

00:27:11.990 --> 00:27:13.670
coming from Google
and Cloud Functions

00:27:13.670 --> 00:27:17.091
with more integrations and
better tools and things

00:27:17.091 --> 00:27:18.965
because we're really
excited about the space.

00:27:18.965 --> 00:27:19.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Awesome.

00:27:19.390 --> 00:27:21.556
And if you want to learn
more about cloud functions,

00:27:21.556 --> 00:27:23.720
we also had an episode
on cloud functions.

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:26.202
Episode number 73, we
have Brett McGowan.

00:27:26.202 --> 00:27:26.952
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

